WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.240
- This chess lawsuit is ass. Or is it?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.470
It all started when 31-year-old

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.330
Grandmaster Magnus Carlsen of Norway,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.130
the top-ranked chess player in the world,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.470
was dealt a rare tournament
defeat by Hans Niemann,

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.530
a 19-year-old American prodigy,
ranked 40th in the world,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.900
who, some have dubbed
the "Bad Boy of Chess",

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.420
due to his oddly combative
demeanor in interviews.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.980
- Well, this is completely
(beep) ridiculous

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.380
that the laptop is just
running outta power.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.000
Like, plug the (beep) laptop in.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.710
- Carlsen then shocked the chess world

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.530
by accusing Neimann of
repeatedly cheating in matches.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.230
These voices only grew
louder after Chess.com,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.020
the world's largest online
chess platform concluded

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.610
after a week's long investigation,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.940
that Neimann had cheated in
more than 100 online games,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.620
then banned him from the platform.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.990
This wild tale has all
the makings of a drama fit

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.340
for a future Netflix series.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.132
Especially given the
salacious Internet rumors

00:00:46.132 --> 00:00:48.090
that Neimann cheated with the help

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.344
of an adult entertainment device

00:00:49.344 --> 00:00:51.960
put in an adult entertainment location.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.460
This method of cheating, of course,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.350
was pioneered by the famous international

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.580
Grandmaster, Ben Wa.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.040
Now, Neimann has vehemently
denied the cheating allegations

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.480
and responded to this controversy the way

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.752
that almost guarantees that
you end up on this channel.

00:01:02.752 --> 00:01:06.300
By filing a $100 million
lawsuit accusing the critics

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.133
of defamation,

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.210
and colluding to blacklist him from play

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.380
and destroy his career.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.570
So let's take a dive into
the Neimann cheating scandal.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.530
We'll break down the claims

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.090
leveled against Neimann, his response,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.860
and whether the $100
million dollar lawsuit

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.027
has any chance of success,

00:01:18.027 --> 00:01:20.760
or whether he's going
to need a silver bullet.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.683
Now, all of this drama
began on September 4th,

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:26.070
when Neimann and Carlsen faced
off at the Sinquefield Cup

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.030
in St. Louis.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.095
Despite being the lowest ranked
player in the tournament,

00:01:29.095 --> 00:01:31.717
Neimann pulled off a stunning
upset against Carlsen

00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:32.910
in the third round,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.820
snapping the world champ's
53-match winning streak.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.970
In a post-match interview,
Neimann was less than gracious.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.770
- I think he's just so demoralized

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.720
because he's losing to such
an idiot like me, you know?

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.760
It's just, it must be embarrassing

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.144
for the world champion to lose to me.

00:01:47.144 --> 00:01:47.977
I feel bad for him.

00:01:47.977 --> 00:01:50.130
- Neimann attributed his
victory to a ridiculous miracle

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.200
that he happened to study
the rare chess opening

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.360
Carlsen used the morning before the game,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.866
allowing Neimann to guess
how Carlsen would start.

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:58.588
- That's them (beep).

00:01:58.588 --> 00:02:00.090
Ah, that's them (beep) right there.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.110
- Carlsen's move the next day

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.180
was an even more unusual variation.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:04.200
Pulling out of the tournament

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.550
for the first time in his career,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.220
and only providing context
with a veiled tweet.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.245
Grandmaster and chess
streamer, Hikaru Nakamura,

00:02:10.245 --> 00:02:13.230
interpreted Carlsen's tweet
as a veiled accusation

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.430
that Neimann was a cheater.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.316
And that this was the reason
for Carlsen's withdrawal,

00:02:16.316 --> 00:02:17.970
after the loss to Neimann.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.526
- At the end of the day, there's nothing.

00:02:19.526 --> 00:02:22.170
There's nothing. Nothing is proven.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.390
But there is a lot of suspicion.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.310
There is a lot of suspicion.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.890
- Neimann has a history of
cheating online on Chess.com,

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.540
and was banned there, twice.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.000
Though Neimann has denied
cheating since his last ban

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.350
when he was 16,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.041
and has stated that he has
never cheated in in-person game,

00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:39.330
or when prize money was involved.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.556
- I've never, ever in
my life cheated in an

00:02:41.556 --> 00:02:43.903
over-the-board game, in online tournament.

00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:45.390
They were an unrated games.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.160
- Following a game on September 19th,

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.516
in which Carlsen resigned
after the first three moves

00:02:49.516 --> 00:02:51.720
in apparent protest, the next day,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.000
Carlsen implicitly accused
Neimann of cheating

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.010
and declared he was unwilling to play

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.290
against people that have
cheated in the past.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.308
Carlsen tweeted that Neimann's

00:02:59.308 --> 00:03:01.620
"Over-the-board progress has been unusual

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.196
and throughout our game
in the Sinquefield Cup,

00:03:03.196 --> 00:03:05.370
I had the impression that he wasn't tense,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.350
or even fully concentrating on the game

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.320
in critical positions, while
outplaying me as black,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.380
in a way I think only a
handful of players can do.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.720
This game contributed to
changing my perspective."

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.220
On October 4th, Chess.com released

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.847
a bombshell 72-page report,

00:03:18.847 --> 00:03:20.880
"Concluded that Neimann
has cheated much more

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.054
than he has publicly admitted to,

00:03:22.054 --> 00:03:25.620
including in many prize events,
at least 25 streamed games,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:28.080
and 100-plus rated games on Chess.com,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.270
as recently as when he was 17 years old."

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.280
These findings contradicted
Neimann's public claims

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.134
that he only cheated twice,

00:03:33.134 --> 00:03:35.430
that he never cheated
in prize money events,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.469
and that he stopped
cheating at the age of 16.

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:39.030
In support of their findings,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.850
Chess.com used a variety of
cheating detection tools,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.130
including a comparative
analysis of the moves made,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.573
versus the moves recommended
by supercomputers,

00:03:46.573 --> 00:03:49.620
a player's past performance
and known strength profile,

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.423
the statistical
significance of the results,

00:03:51.423 --> 00:03:54.015
and input from grandmaster analysis.

00:03:54.015 --> 00:03:57.510
While Chess.com concedes that
"there was no direct evidence"

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.115
that Neimann cheated in his
win at the Sinquefield Cup,

00:04:00.115 --> 00:04:03.120
the report did highlight
some suspicious elements.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.680
Specifically, the report
addressed Neimann's

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.810
apparent struggle to discuss the rationale

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:07.980
of his own chess strategy

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.080
after beating Carlsen in St. Louis,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.540
which top players at the time described as

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.460
at odds with the level of preparation

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.160
that Hans claimed was at play in the game,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.470
and the level of analysis needed to defeat

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.730
the world chess champion.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.610
But, one might ask, how does
a person even cheat at chess?

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:24.810
Before this story broke,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.167
you might have been
surprised to see the words

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.750
"chess" and "cheating"
in the same sentence.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.437
But in the words of the Washington Post,

00:04:29.437 --> 00:04:31.740
"Chess has long been a game rife

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.080
with allegations of
chicanery in skullduggery."

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.610
- This, this chicanery!

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.230
- As technology has advanced, so too,

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.546
have chess cheating methodologies.

00:04:38.546 --> 00:04:40.858
These days, people have been
caught using chess engines,

00:04:40.858 --> 00:04:43.260
computer programs that analyze games

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.390
to recommend the strongest possible moves.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.090
Chess engines are so
advanced that they can beat

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.249
even the best grandmasters,
every single time.

00:04:50.249 --> 00:04:53.340
- Mate, in 143 moves.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.220
- No, pooh, you win again!

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.367
- And in a video entitled,

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:58.879
"Is This Proof that Hans Neimann Cheated?"

00:04:58.879 --> 00:05:01.050
Nakamura reacted to a video

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.841
from French streamer, Yosha Iglésias,

00:05:02.841 --> 00:05:06.360
discussing a third-party
statistical analysis of 10 games,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.070
where Neimann's chess play achieved

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.840
a near 100 percent correlation

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.360
to the perfect moves of a chess engine.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.284
According to the analysis,
no other players,

00:05:14.284 --> 00:05:17.940
not even Carlsen, boasts
so many 100 percent games.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.560
And recently, players
have been caught cheating

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.570
using chess engines on their smartphones.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.330
Like Georgian Grandmaster Gaioz Nigalidze,

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.220
who, at 25 years old, was caught cheating

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.530
at the 2015 Dubai Open,

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.140
using a chess engine on
the smartphone he hid

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.190
in the bathroom stall.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.260
Nigalidze's Grandmaster title was revoked

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.420
and he was banned from
competition for three years.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.639
But what happens if you
can't access your smartphone?

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:41.010
Well, one particularly
salacious rumor began

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:41.970
to circulate on Reddit

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.310
that Neimann could have
cheated using a well-placed

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.890
Wi-Fi connected vibrating
adult entertainment device.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:47.760
Now, to be clear,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.820
there is absolutely zero
evidence that this happened.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.442
It's just a Reddit rumor.

00:05:52.442 --> 00:05:55.680
Though, it is technically
possible to cheat this way.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.513
But for more on this,

00:05:56.513 --> 00:05:58.733
let's go to one of YouTube's
resident chess experts

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.270
at Gotham Chess,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.000
to explain more about
these cheating allegations.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.820
- Hey everyone, I'm Levy Rozman,
also known as Gotham Chess.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.920
And I've got the largest
chess channel here on YouTube.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.350
Thanks so much, Legal Eagle,
for featuring me in this video.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.840
I'll try to answer all the
questions as best as I can,

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.340
even though I'm here in a hotel room.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.500
So, I'm sorry if the setup
isn't as good as yours.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.780
There have been players in the
past, like Barislov Ivanov,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.690
who was found with a device in his shoe.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.880
That device was somehow
registering good moves

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:25.800
and vibrating to indicate to him

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.150
something about the chess board.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.100
Maybe it was Morse code,
maybe it was something else.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.030
There was another case.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.850
I believe it was the French
Olympic team in 2010.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.530
There was a whole elaborate system.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.600
Somebody sitting at home

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.712
was watching the game live, broadcasted.

00:06:38.712 --> 00:06:42.480
That person was then relaying
something to a middle man,

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.580
who was then standing in a certain corner

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.260
of the room itself,

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.790
to relay information to the person

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:48.810
that was playing at the board.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.970
- Let's think about a very
simple representation.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.372
I would need two squares.
- [Off-Screen Voice] Mm hmm.

00:06:53.372 --> 00:06:56.820
- The first, to designate which piece,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.770
and the second, where the piece is moving.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:06:59.987
That's probably the easiest.

00:06:59.987 --> 00:07:03.210
What's the smallest amount of information

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.220
you need to help you?

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.620
- Basically like a buzz,
in a critical position.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.453
That would be good.

00:07:08.453 --> 00:07:09.570
- And what would the buzz say?

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.500
- It would, it'd basically,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.570
it'd be something like one
buzz means the position

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.780
is great, and two buzzes means
the position is completely,

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:16.650
it's completely equal.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.420
Or there's nothing
special in the position.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.580
- Now, Neimann vehemently
denies the cheating allegations,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.980
even colorfully offering
to strip naked before games

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.360
to prove he's not hiding anything.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.950
- They could literally tell
me the most ridiculous thing.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.870
They want me to strip fully naked.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.130
I'll do it, I don't care.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.431
- [Host] And well, so far
he's not played in the nude.

00:07:31.431 --> 00:07:32.864
He, along with the other players,

00:07:32.864 --> 00:07:35.430
was subject to a full body
scan with a metal detector

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.970
at the US Chess Championship in St. Louis.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.590
Which takes us to October 20th,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.470
when Neimann made his next move
by filing a federal lawsuit

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.477
against Magnus Carlsen,
Carlsen's chess company,

00:07:46.477 --> 00:07:49.200
"Play Magnus", Nakamura, Chess.com,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.203
and Chess.com executive, Danny Rensch.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:54.150
The complaint demands
at least $100 million

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.500
in damages from the defendants,

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:56.652
arising out of causes of action

00:07:56.652 --> 00:08:00.120
for slander, libel, unlawful group boycott

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.378
under the Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:08:01.378 --> 00:08:03.988
tortious interference
with Neimann's business,

00:08:03.988 --> 00:08:05.730
and civil conspiracy.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.950
The 44-page complaint is
primarily a recounting

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.679
of public statements made by
Carlsen, Rensch and Nakamura.

00:08:10.679 --> 00:08:13.260
Though it notably does
not address any salacious,

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.210
or unsubstantiated Internet rumors.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.580
Neimann asserts that there is
no truth to the allegations

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.653
that he cheated at the
Sinquefield Cup, claiming that

00:08:19.653 --> 00:08:22.650
"Unbiased top chess
analysts have concluded

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.180
that his victory resulted more

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.130
from Carlsen's particularly poor play,

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:27.630
than Neimann's exceptional play."

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.850
The complaint also calls
Chess.com's cheating report

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.994
a "false and malicious hatchet job

00:08:31.994 --> 00:08:33.840
full of slanted analysis."

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.070
And that the report's statement

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.780
of Neimann's privately confessing

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:40.115
to additional cheating offenses
to Danny Rensch, is false.

00:08:40.115 --> 00:08:42.264
The complaint is full of effusive praise

00:08:42.264 --> 00:08:46.050
for the "American chess
prodigy, Hans Neimann",

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.140
and vitriolic insults
for the other defendants.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.217
For example, the lawsuit claims that a

00:08:51.217 --> 00:08:54.240
"corrupt and cowardly"
Carlsen chose to viciously

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.517
and maliciously defame Neimann

00:08:55.517 --> 00:08:57.300
with false cheating allegations,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.970
due to his "inability to cope with defeat"

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:00.990
at the Sinquefield Cup,

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:02.681
after a man 12 years his junior

00:09:02.681 --> 00:09:05.760
"dared to disrespect the King of Chess."

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.650
The complaint accuses Nakamura of joining

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.450
in this defamatory conspiracy

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.107
because he viewed Neimann as a

00:09:11.107 --> 00:09:12.720
"threat to Nakamura's dominance

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.250
on chess streaming platforms."

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.080
The complaint also accuses Chess.com

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.250
of colluding with Carlsen

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.777
because the company plans
to purchase Carlsen's

00:09:19.777 --> 00:09:23.040
"Play Magnus" app for nearly $83 million,

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.670
in a merger Neimann claims will
monopolize the chess world.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.650
Though this merger was
announced in August,

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.120
before the cheating allegations.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.920
Neimann also alleges
the defendants violated

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.810
the Sherman Antitrust Act of 1890,

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.954
which outlaws certain
monopolistic business practices

00:09:35.954 --> 00:09:39.030
by colluding to blacklist
Neimann with false

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.770
and defamatory cheating allegations

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:44.100
that "by design, destroyed
Neimann's remarkable career

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.840
in its prime and ruined his life."

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.137
Neimann also alleges that

00:09:47.137 --> 00:09:50.100
"defendants acted in concert
to improperly refuse to deal

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:52.500
with Neimann, including
Chess.com banning Neimann

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.115
from its platform, and sponsored events,

00:09:54.115 --> 00:09:56.070
Carlsen refusing to play Neimann

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.702
in any tournaments or events

00:09:57.702 --> 00:10:00.467
and defendants acting
collectively to cause organizers

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:03.420
of professional chess
tournaments to blacklist Neimann

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.370
from participating in their events."

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.970
So, the $100 million question.
Can Neimann win this case?

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.111
Well, among legal observers,

00:10:10.111 --> 00:10:11.580
the emerging consensus is

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.320
that Neimann faces an uphill battle.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.120
So let's start with the prayer for relief.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.490
Neimann seeks $100 million in damages.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.505
And while that's certainly
a jaw-dropping number,

00:10:19.505 --> 00:10:22.560
it has zero basis in reality.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.660
He certainly pulled
that one out of his ass.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:25.950
Whenever someone files a lawsuit,

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:27.660
they can put any number they want.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.190
And in a complaint like this,

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.892
that serves, potentially,
to recover damages,

00:10:31.892 --> 00:10:33.269
but also serves to try

00:10:33.269 --> 00:10:35.635
and rehabilitate someone's reputation,

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:39.420
it's there to grab headlines,
which it certainly did.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.600
But in a case like this,
any prayer for relief,

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.457
whether it's $1 or $100 million dollars,

00:10:44.457 --> 00:10:46.468
means absolutely nothing.

00:10:46.468 --> 00:10:49.770
But let's move on to the
rest of this lawsuit,

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:51.540
and start with jurisdiction.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.480
Because Neimann has to
show why federal courts

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.840
in St. Louis even have
jurisdiction to decide this matter.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.090
And the fact that Neimann
filed suit in Missouri

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.710
raises all kinds of questions.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.450
To hear a case, a court has to have

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.760
both personal jurisdiction, and
subject matter jurisdiction.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.470
Now, subject matter
jurisdiction is the requirement

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:10.107
that a court has the power to
hear a specific kind of claim.

00:11:10.107 --> 00:11:11.760
And for the moment, it
seems that the courts

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.920
having federal jurisdiction
might make sense

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.263
because the complaint at
the moment asserts a claim

00:11:16.263 --> 00:11:18.570
for a violation of the
Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.618
which is a federal law.

00:11:19.618 --> 00:11:23.250
And there might be something
called diversity jurisdiction,

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.680
given the residency of
all of the defendants.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.990
But there's also the personal
jurisdiction requirement

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.010
that a court has power
over the defendants,

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.837
based on usually what's called

00:11:31.837 --> 00:11:33.600
"minimum contacts with that forum."

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.550
And the most common way to
get personal jurisdiction

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:36.960
over a person, or a business,

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.520
is to sue them where they live,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.340
or do business, or
where the tort occurred.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.350
But here, it's entirely
possible that some,

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.420
or all the defendants could
get this suit dismissed,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.680
at least without prejudice.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.014
Meaning that the suit could be refiled,

00:11:48.014 --> 00:11:50.956
given their lack of
connection to St. Louis.

00:11:50.956 --> 00:11:53.040
Now, Neimann lives in Connecticut.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.300
Nakamura lives in Florida.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.250
Carlsen and his company are from Norway,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.740
and Chess.com is a Nevada LLC,

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.390
while Rensch lives in Utah.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.670
The only one with any links
to Missouri is Carlsen,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.000
who allegedly made the defamatory
remarks that first arose

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.696
out of his defeat in the
Sinquefield Cup in St. Louis.

00:12:08.696 --> 00:12:11.430
So why would Neimann sue in Missouri,

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.110
instead of his home in Connecticut?

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:14.400
Well, some have speculated that it's

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.852
because of Connecticut's
strong anti-SLAPP statute,

00:12:16.852 --> 00:12:19.410
which dismisses lawsuits
that are brought simply

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:22.129
to retaliate against defendants'
exercising of free speech.

00:12:22.129 --> 00:12:24.149
Which would've required
Neimann to show evidence

00:12:24.149 --> 00:12:26.643
for every single element of his claims

00:12:26.643 --> 00:12:29.430
against every single
defendant to avoid dismissal.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:30.263
And in contrast,

00:12:30.263 --> 00:12:32.460
Missouri's anti-SLAPP laws
basically non-existent,

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.385
and it only applies to
comments at public hearings.

00:12:34.385 --> 00:12:36.660
Which certainly wouldn't
stop Neimann's suit.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.402
So let's talk about
Neimann's defamation claims.

00:12:38.402 --> 00:12:41.166
If you haven't seen are
dozens of videos tackling

00:12:41.166 --> 00:12:43.110
the topic of defamation.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:45.030
Defamation is a tort that
creates civil liability

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.586
for false statements that injure
a third party's reputation.

00:12:47.586 --> 00:12:50.160
Neimann has sued the
defendants for slander,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.130
which is verbal defamation,
and libel, written defamation.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.380
But there's several
barriers that could make

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.610
these claims hard to win,

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.550
even if the cheating allegations
are proven to be untrue.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.350
For starters, defamation
is only actionable

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.842
if a defendant makes a false
statement of verifiable fact,

00:13:03.842 --> 00:13:05.940
meaning the assertion must be capable

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:08.550
of definitively being
proven true, or false.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:10.860
And opinions, are by
nature, not defamatory,

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.750
if they merely express
these speakers' commentary,

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.250
based on public information.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.400
And here, Carlsen accusing
Neimann of cheating after losing

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.430
at the Sinquefield Cup is
probably not an assertion of fact,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.610
but a opinion based on disclosed fact.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.443
And as some have said,

00:13:24.443 --> 00:13:26.473
"It may be a stupid opinion.
It may be a wrong opinion.

00:13:26.473 --> 00:13:27.690
But it's an opinion,

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.390
a conclusion reached based
on the observed play,

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.970
not a statement of fact.
It can't be defamatory."

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:34.590
And to prevail, Neimann
would have to prove

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:36.871
that Carlsen didn't actually
think Neimann cheated

00:13:36.871 --> 00:13:38.143
when he made those claims.

00:13:38.143 --> 00:13:40.920
A very high, if not
impossible bar to clear.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.770
Similarly, Nakamura may be
shielded from defamation claims

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.930
to the extent that he's
only giving his opinion

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.600
and commentary on
Neimann's alleged cheating,

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.497
based on public facts.

00:13:49.497 --> 00:13:52.320
And not to fear, the same
goes for a legal commentary,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:53.965
YouTube channel giving legal commentary

00:13:53.965 --> 00:13:55.620
on a chess cheating scandal.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:58.710
But the complaint does suggest
that Chess.com's conclusion

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:01.710
that Neimann cheated, is
based on undisclosed facts.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:04.230
And sometimes implying undisclosed facts,

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.600
or explicitly stating that
there are undisclosed facts,

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.430
can lead to defamation.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.110
And the Chess.com report alleges

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:11.910
that Neimann "privately confessed

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:13.380
to additional cheating offenses",

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:15.270
in a private August, 2020 phone call

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.502
with Chess.com's, Danny Rensch,

00:14:16.502 --> 00:14:18.240
which the complaint denies.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.410
However, the Chess.com report

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:20.910
does include private communications

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.520
between Neimann and Rensch,
between August, 2020

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.790
and January, 2021, that shows
the Zoom call took place.

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:28.696
Neimann acknowledging
being banned for cheating,

00:14:28.696 --> 00:14:31.234
and calling said consequences
"completely fair."

00:14:31.234 --> 00:14:33.960
And that's certainly going to
hurt Neimann's credibility,

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.180
unless he can show that those
private messages were faked,

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.440
or taken out of context.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:38.805
Another potential barrier to recovery,

00:14:38.805 --> 00:14:40.860
Neimann's fame and notoriety means

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.350
that he could be classified
as a public figure,

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.080
subject to the heightened
actual malice standard.

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:47.820
To prove actual malice, which, again,

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.460
has nothing to do with hating someone,

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.110
one must prove that the
statements were made

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:53.730
with knowledge of being false,

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:56.340
or reckless disregard for
their truth or falsity.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.110
The actual malice standard also applies

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:59.288
to limited purpose public figures,

00:14:59.288 --> 00:15:01.980
which refers to one who
voluntarily participates

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.800
in a discussion about a
particular controversy

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:08.426
and has access to the media to
get his, or her views across.

00:15:08.426 --> 00:15:10.560
This is opposed to the normal standard,

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.629
which is just negligence with
respect to false statements.

00:15:13.629 --> 00:15:16.110
But Neimann's almost-Trumpian praise

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.360
of how famous and successful he is,

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.040
that's contained in the complaint,

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.590
only reinforces the idea
that he should be treated

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.290
as a public figure, subject
to the actual malice standard.

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:26.490
But that's a double-edged sword,

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.993
because this complaint is
potentially to redress grievances

00:15:29.993 --> 00:15:32.970
in court, and get damages.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.070
But it's also supposed
to try to rehabilitate

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.340
Neimann's own reputation,
and get eyeballs on it.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:39.920
So, it goes both ways.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:42.558
But, given this high standard of proof,

00:15:42.558 --> 00:15:46.260
Neimann might not have any luck
against Chess.com in court.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.057
And as Bloomberg columnist
Stephen L. Carter points out,

00:15:49.057 --> 00:15:51.000
"lawsuits by individuals
accused of cheating,

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.280
have mostly been unsuccessful with courts

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.900
often unwilling to second guess

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.810
a gaming organization's own
findings of wrongdoing."

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.510
As an example, Carter cites
to a 2010 Indiana case,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:03.156
where a golf teaching pro
unsuccessfully sued a colleague

00:16:03.156 --> 00:16:05.040
for calling him "a cheat"

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:07.110
for claiming a different classification

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:09.480
than the one to which he was entitled.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.850
In that case, the Professional
Golf Association agreed

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:15.270
with the colleague's allegations
following an investigation

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.070
and the court dismissed
the defamation claim

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:18.750
because the statements were true.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.270
As Carter notes, the court
didn't do its own investigation.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:23.880
Rather, the organization's
own judgment was sufficient

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.950
to shield the defendant from liability.

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:28.170
And doubly so, it's
hard to win an antitrust

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.570
and intentional interference
claim in the absence

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.730
of prevailing on a defamation claim.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:34.560
And those remaining causes of action

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:35.910
are also not particularly strong,

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:37.770
based on the allegations
made in the complaint.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.780
For antitrust claims, in this context,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:41.816
it's likely that Neimann would
have to plead enough facts

00:16:41.816 --> 00:16:43.920
to convince a jury that if true,

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.470
the defendants entered
into an actual agreement

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:47.550
to blacklist Neimann.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.070
But Neimann and Chess.com
are not competitors,

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.869
and complaining that Chess.com and Carlsen

00:16:51.869 --> 00:16:54.870
won't voluntarily play with him,

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.600
doesn't necessarily establish
an illegal agreement.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.400
And simply alleging that the defendants

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.050
did similar things, at the same time,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:03.990
isn't enough to allege an
agreement in restraint of trade.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:06.420
And additionally, tortious
interference allows for a claim

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.508
for damages when a defendant
wrongfully interferes

00:17:08.508 --> 00:17:11.730
with an existing contractual,
or business relationship.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:14.070
The key there being wrongful interference

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:16.260
with an existing contractual relationship.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.180
The complaint makes conclusory allegations

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.650
that the defendants
intentionally interfered

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.420
with Neimann's relationship with a series

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:22.553
of chess-related entities,

00:17:22.553 --> 00:17:25.830
to coerce them to terminate
the relationships with him,

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.510
and blacklist him from future trade.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:28.860
But these are tough claims to make,

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.720
because the complaint
doesn't actually allege

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.810
any conduct directed at these entities.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.480
And to prevail on a civil
conspiracy cause of action,

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:38.880
a plaintiff has to prove that
two or more defendants, again,

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.740
entered into an agreement
to commit a wrong,

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.320
and did actually commit a
tortious act in furtherance

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.450
of that agreement that caused
injury to a third party.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.370
And Neimann makes conclusory allegations

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.920
that the defendants agreed to
do bad stuff to hurt Neimann,

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.320
but doesn't actually
provide the factual basis

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:53.910
suggesting an agreement to do that.

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:55.597
In quoting Texas Judge John Chupp,

00:17:55.597 --> 00:17:57.660
"Three people all liking
the same cat picture

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.310
on the Internet is not a conspiracy."

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.140
But the thing about real conspiracies

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:02.577
is that you rarely have evidence

00:18:02.577 --> 00:18:04.807
of that agreement at face value.

00:18:04.807 --> 00:18:07.710
So, if this lawsuit does
make it past the motion

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:10.140
to dismiss stage, into discovery,

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.630
I'm sure the very first thing
that Neimann will ask for,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:14.820
are all communications
related to agreements

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.620
between all of these parties.

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:19.470
And who knows, maybe
those agreements exist.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:20.970
But to understand whether Neimann cheated

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.184
or whether Carlsen is being a sore loser,

00:18:23.184 --> 00:18:24.900
it would really help to
understand statistics

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:25.733
and probability.

